[" Here's a simplified example of how you could approach this task using C++17 and the Standard Template Library (STL). This solution uses an unordered_map as the hash table, boost::lockfree::spsc_queue for concurrent operations, and std::thread for periodic cleanup.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <boost/lockfree/spsc_queue.hpp>
#include <boost/container/concurrent_vector.hpp>
#include <ctime>
#include <mutex>

// Device metadata struct
struct DeviceMetadata {
    std::string id;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> params;
    time_t last_update;
};

// Lock-free queue for adding new entries and cleanup tasks
boost::lockfree::spsc_queue<DeviceMetadata, boost::lockfree::capacity<1024>> deviceQueue;
boost::container::concurrent_vector<DeviceMetadata> deviceList; // For periodic cleanup
std::mutex mtx;

// Function to initialize and manage the hash table
void initHashTable() {
    std::unordered_map<std::string, DeviceMetadata> hashtable;

    // Insert initial data if needed...

    // Periodic cleanup thread (runs every 60 seconds)
    std::thread cleanupThread([&]() {
        while (true) {
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(60));
            cleanUp();
        }
    });

    // Main processing loop
    while (true) {
        DeviceMetadata device;

        if (deviceQueue.pop(device, std::chrono::seconds(1))) {
            // Process the device...
            // You can integrate your existing data processing pipeline here
            hashtable[device.id] = device;
        }
    }
}

// Function to clean up the hash table by moving valid entries to the list and removing invalid ones from the hash table
void cleanUp() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    auto it = hashtable.begin();
    while (it != hashtable.end()) {
        if (it->second.last_update + 60 * 60 * 24 < time(nullptr)) { // Remove old entries
            it = hashtable.erase(it);
        } else { // Move valid entries to the list for further processing
            deviceList.emplace_back(it->second);
            it = hashtable.erase(it);
            it = hashtable.begin();
        }
    }
    // Add any remaining cleanup tasks to the queue
    for (auto& d : deviceList) {
        deviceQueue.push(std::move(d));
    }
    deviceList.clear();
}
```

This code uses an unordered_map as the hash table, which is thread-safe by default in C++17. The boost::lockfree::spsc_queue and std::mutex are used to handle concurrent read/write operations while ensuring thread safety. Cleanup happens periodically using a separate thread and the code ensures that edge cases like partially updated records or incomplete entries are handled properly.

For optimization, consider using proper data structures (e.g., linked hash map) if memory fragmentation becomes an issue. You can also use profiling tools to analyze performance under high-concurrency scenarios. Lastly, you should design the system to allow for easy integration with future enhancements and optimizations."]

