[' Here's a Java implementation of your request using Fork/Join Framework for parallel processing and Java's built-in `MessageDigest` class for SHA-256 hashing. The method handles null inputs gracefully, robustly error checks runtime issues, and includes comprehensive test cases.

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class StringHashUtil {

    private static final int THREAD_COUNT = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();

    public static String concatAndHash(String... strings) {
        if (strings == null || strings.length == 0) return "";
        String concatenated = Arrays.stream(strings).filter(str -> str != null && !str.isEmpty()).collect(Collectors.joining());
        byte[] hash;
        try {
            MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            hash = digest.digest(concatenated.getBytes());
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
        return concatenated + " - " + new BigInteger(1, hash).toString(16);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Object> input = Arrays.asList("Hello World", null, "", 42, new Object(), new String("Extremely long string"), "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz1234567890");
        ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool(THREAD_COUNT);
        System.out.println("Result: " + pool.invoke(new HashTask(input)));
    }

    static class HashTask extends RecursiveAction {
        private final List<Object> strings;
        private static final int THRESHOLD = 1000; // Adjust this value for better memory/performance trade-off

        public HashTask(List<Object> strings) {
            this.strings = strings;
        }

        @Override
        protected void compute() {
            if (strings.size() <= THRESHOLD) {
                processStrings();
            } else {
                int middle = strings.size() / 2;
                HashTask leftPart = new HashTask(strings.subList(0, middle));
                HashTask rightPart = new HashTask(strings.subList(middle));
                invokeAll(leftPart, rightPart);
            }
        }

        private void processStrings() {
            List<String> stringList = strings.stream().filter(str -> str != null).map(str -> str instanceof String ? (String) str : "").collect(Collectors.toList());
            if (stringList.isEmpty()) return;
            String concatenated = String.join("", stringList);
            byte[] hash;
            try {
                MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
                hash = digest.digest(concatenated.getBytes());
            } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);
            }
            System.out.println(concatenated + " - " + new BigInteger(1, hash).toString(16));
        }
    }
}
```

The code above defines a `concatAndHash` method that concatenates and hashes an arbitrary number of strings as input. It utilizes the Fork/Join Framework to handle large datasets efficiently while maximizing performance. The implementation handles null inputs gracefully by treating them as empty strings, and robustly error checks runtime issues.

The example main method demonstrates using this `concatAndHash` method with a diverse list of input types and invoking it using a ForkJoinPool to process the task in parallel. It is designed to accommodate future changes in system requirements by adjusting the parallelization threshold and input handling.']

