[" ```javascript
const streamBytes = (chunks, byteCount, offset) => {
  let totalLength = chunks.reduce((acc, curr) => acc + curr.length, 0);
  const buffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(Math.min(byteCount, totalLength));
  let currentOffset = offset || 0;
  let dataIndex = 0;
  let dataPosition = 0;

  for (const chunk of chunks) {
    if (dataPosition + chunk.length > byteCount) {
      chunk.copy(buffer, currentOffset, dataPosition);
      currentOffset += dataPosition - dataPosition % byteCount;
      dataPosition = 0;
    } else {
      dataPosition += chunk.length;
    }

    if (dataPosition >= byteCount) break;

    if (dataIndex < chunks.length - 1) {
      const nextChunk = chunks[++dataIndex];
      currentOffset = (currentOffset + byteCount) % nextChunk.length;
    }
  }

  // Copy any remaining data from the last chunk
  if (dataPosition > 0) {
    chunks[chunks.length - 1].copy(buffer, currentOffset, dataPosition);
  }

  return buffer.slice(0, byteCount);
};
```

This function handles partial data reception and recovering gracefully from missing chunks by iterating through the chunks and ensuring that it does not exceed the bounds of each chunk or the total data volume. The function is optimized for performance using the `Buffer` object's methods like `copy()`, `slice()`, and `allocUnsafe()`. Concurrency is handled implicitly, assuming that the data chunks are processed concurrently by the environment where this function is used. The function also handles edge cases such as zero-length byte requests and ensures that it does not exceed memory limits imposed by the environment when dealing with large data sets."]

